1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
HAZAL:Tom, could I ask you for some advice, please?

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,223
Tom:Yes of course, if you think I can help.

3
00:00:08,223 --> 00:00:09,340
What's it about?

4
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:11,683
HAZAL:It's my first media studies assignment,

5
00:00:11,683 --> 00:00:14,697
and I'm not sure how to go about it.

6
00:00:14,697 --> 00:00:17,040
You must have done it last year.

7
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:22,140
Tom:Is that the one comparing the coverage of a particular story in a range

8
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:22,820
of newspapers?

9
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:23,730
HAZAL:That's right.

10
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:26,940
Tom:Oh yes, I really enjoyed writing it.

11
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:30,130
HAZAL:So what sort of things do I need to compare?

12
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:32,407
Tom:Well, there are several things.

13
00:00:32,407 --> 00:00:37,720
For example, there's the question of which page of the newspaper the item appears

14
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:38,100
on.

15
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:39,138
Hazel:You mean,

16
00:00:39,138 --> 00:00:43,291
because there's a big difference between having it on the front page

17
00:00:43,291 --> 00:00:46,060
and the bottom of page ten, for instance?

18
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:46,905
Tom:Exactly.

19
00:00:46,905 --> 00:00:51,550
And that shows how important the editor thinks the story is.

20
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:57,934
Then there's the size – how many column inches the story is given, how

21
00:00:57,934 --> 00:01:00,390
many columns it spreads over.

22
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:03,730
Hazel:And I suppose that includes the headline.

23
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:05,100
Tom:It certainly does.

24
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:07,840
It's all part of attracting the reader's attention.

25
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:13,430
Hazel:What about graphics – whether there's anything visual in addition to the text?

26
00:01:13,430 --> 00:01:15,920
Tom:Yes, you need to consider those, too,

27
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:20,845
because they can have a big effect on the reader's understanding of the story

28
00:01:20,845 --> 00:01:23,660
– sometimes a bigger effect than the text itself.

29
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:28,199
Then you'll need to look at how the item is put together:

30
00:01:28,199 --> 00:01:30,090
what structure is it given?

31
00:01:30,090 --> 00:01:36,060
Bear in mind that not many people read beyond the first paragraph,

32
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:38,940
so what has the journalist put at the beginning?

33
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:44,927
And if, say, there are conflicting opinions about something, does one appear near the

34
00:01:44,927 --> 00:01:47,920
end, where people probably won't read it?

35
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:53,730
Hazel:And newspapers sometimes give wrong or misleading information, don't they?

36
00:01:53,730 --> 00:01:55,880
Either deliberately or by accident.

37
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:58,890
Should I be looking at that, too?

38
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:00,823
Tom:Yes, if you can.

39
00:02:00,823 --> 00:02:02,757
Compare what's in different versions,

40
00:02:02,757 --> 00:02:08,170
and as far as possible, try and work out what's true and what isn't.

41
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:12,490
And that relates to a very important point:

42
00:02:12,490 --> 00:02:14,650
what's the writer's purpose,

43
00:02:14,650 --> 00:02:19,160
or at least the most important one, if they have several.

44
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:22,630
It may seem to be to inform the public,

45
00:02:22,630 --> 00:02:26,100
but often it's that they want to create fear,

46
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:27,110
or controversy,

47
00:02:27,110 --> 00:02:30,140
or to make somebody look ridiculous.

48
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:32,788
Hazel:Gosh, I see what you mean.

49
00:02:32,788 --> 00:02:36,950
And I suppose the writer may make assumptions about the reader.

50
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:41,357
Tom:That's right – about their knowledge of the subject, their attitudes,

51
00:02:41,357 --> 00:02:43,360
and their level of education,

52
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:48,830
which means writing so that the readers understand without feeling patronised.

53
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:53,070
All of that will make a difference to how the story is presented.

54
00:02:53,070 --> 00:02:59,680
Hazel:Does it matter what type of story I write about?

55
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:03,381
Tom:No – national or international politics, the arts ...

56
00:03:03,381 --> 00:03:08,470
Anything, as long as it's covered in two or three newspapers.

57
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:13,712
Though of course it'll be easier and more fun if it's something you're interested in

58
00:03:13,712 --> 00:03:15,110
and know something about.

59
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:20,457
Hazel:And on that basis a national news item would be worth analysing – I'm

60
00:03:20,457 --> 00:03:21,985
quite keen on politics,

61
00:03:21,985 --> 00:03:25,040
so I'll try and find a suitable topic.

62
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:28,550
What did you choose for your analysis, Tom?

63
00:03:28,550 --> 00:03:33,990
Tom:I was interested in how newspapers express their opinions explicitly,

64
00:03:33,990 --> 00:03:38,458
so I wanted to compare editorials in different papers,

65
00:03:38,458 --> 00:03:40,940
but when I started looking,

66
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:45,140
I couldn't find two on the same topic that I felt like analysing.

67
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:47,990
Hazel:In that case, I won't even bother to look.

68
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:52,427
Tom:So in the end I chose a human interest story – a terribly emotional

69
00:03:52,427 --> 00:03:55,280
story about a young girl who was very ill,

70
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:59,437
and lots of other people – mostly strangers – raised money

71
00:03:59,437 --> 00:04:02,670
so she could go abroad for treatment.

72
00:04:02,670 --> 00:04:08,850
Actually, I was surprised – some papers just wrote about how wonderful everyone was,

73
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:15,060
but others considered the broader picture, like why treatment wasn't available here.

74
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:20,770
Hazel:Hmm, I usually find stories like that raise quite strong feelings in me!

75
00:04:20,770 --> 00:04:22,410
I'll avoid that.

76
00:04:22,410 --> 00:04:27,355
Perhaps I'll choose an arts topic, like different reviews of a film,

77
00:04:27,355 --> 00:04:30,240
or something about funding for the arts –

78
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:31,880
I'll think about that.

79
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:32,600
tom:Yes,

80
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:34,040
that might be interesting.

81
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:37,026
Hazel:OK, well thanks a lot for your help, Tom.

82
00:04:37,026 --> 00:04:38,220
It's been really useful.

83
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:39,243
Tom:You're welcome.

84
00:04:39,243 --> 00:04:41,290
Good luck with the assignment, Hazel.
